 a run of trailing 9s. Find the first non-9 from the right, bump it, and zero everything after it.**\n\nScan from the last digit. A digit below 9 absorbs the carry: increment it and return immediately. A 9 becomes 0 and the carry moves left. If the loop finishes, all digits were 9 and the result is `[1, 0, 0, ..., 0]` with one extra digit.\n\n**Why it is correct:** this is exactly column addition of `+1`: the carry is 1 while we see 9s and becomes 0 at the first smaller digit, after which no digit to the left changes.\n\n**Common mistakes:** converting to an integer and back (defeats the purpose and overflows in fixed-width languages); handling the all-9 case by inserting at index 0 of a long list in a loop (quadratic); mutating the input when the caller may not expect it (clarify, or copy).\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** (1) Add an arbitrary number `k` instead of 1 (carry becomes `k % 10`-style, see Add to Array-Form of Integer). (2) Add two digit arrays (Add Strings / Add Binary). (3) Digits stored in a linked list, least significant first or most significant first (Add Two Numbers I/II).","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1) extra, O(n) only when the array grows"},"similar":["add-binary","add-strings","multiply-strings"],"number":164,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def plusOne(self, digits: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        pass\n"}